PythonModule
PythonModule
Use the object PythonModule for utilizing Python code in a Plant Simulation model with full access to the
Plant Simulation object model from within the Python code.
Image
Requirements
Using Python requires an installation of CPython on your computer.
We recommend using the official Python distribution from https://www.python.org/. You can also use a
Python version that you installed via Anaconda.
If you are using multiple Python environments, specify the path to the Python DLL with the SimTalk
function setPythonDLLPath:
setPythonDLLPath(dllpath:string)
You might, for example, type:
setPythonDLLPath("C:\Program Files\Python312\python312.dll")
Note
Plant Simulation supports Python versions 3.12, 3.13, and 3.14.
PythonModule
11-3724 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Limitations When Using Python Libraries
When you use certain libraries, like Pandas or NumPy, you should always exit Plant Simulation instead
of only closing your simulation model and opening a new model. When you close the model, the Python
interpreter is uninitialized. If you then open a new model and try to use Pandas or NumPy again, this might
cause a crash because these libraries do not support reinitialization of the Python interpreter.
Python libraries like MatPlotLib can open a window from within the Python code where a new Windows
message pump is started. You must close such a window before you can interact with Plant Simulation
again, otherwise Plant Simulation might crash.
To show a tooltip with information about the PythonModule, hover with the mouse over it.
Adding the PythonModule to Your Model
To add the PythonModule to the Class Library, click 
  Manage Class Library > Basic Objects >
Information Flow > PythonModule on the Home ribbon tab.
PythonModule
