What You Can Do With the PythonModule
Python is a high-level programming language. It provides a vast amount of libraries and use cases for your
use. There is not much that you cannot do with Python.
Remarks
You might, for example, use the PythonModule to extract simulation results, do extensive data processing
using any of the available Python libraries, and display the values in different ways by creating
presentations containing various types of charts. You don't even have to know the programming language
Python as such, as ChatGPT is really well-trained for Python.
What You Can Do With the PythonModule
11-3728 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

To import the module into the Python interpreter, click Import in the top right corner of the PythonModule
dialog.
You can also press F5 while the input cursor is located on the Tab Content:
Note
Plant Simulation automatically applies the source code, when you click Import or press F5.
The PythonModule provides the built-in attribute PythonCode of data type string to get and set the Python
source code from a SimTalk Method.
To run the Python code from SimTalk, we provide the methods call and callKw. The respective SimTalk
syntax looks like this:
<Path>.call([functionName:string, ...]) -> any
<Path>.callKw([functionName:string, ..., KeyValuePairs:any[] ]) -> any
To execute a function, which you defined in the PythonModule, specify the name of the function as the
first argument and then the arguments which you want to pass to Python.
Let’s assume that you have the following Python code in your PythonModule:
def multiply(a, b):
return a*b
What You Can Do With the PythonModule
Plant Simulation Help 11-3729
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

Click Apply in the dialog and then execute the function from SimTalk in a Method with print.
Note
Make sure that Prohibit Access to the Computer in the Model Settings is cleared.
As the error messages that Python shows are in English, Plant Simulation shows them in English as well.
We do not localize Python error messages to other languages.
print MyPythonModule.call("multiply", 3, 4)
The result looks like this when we run the Method.
What You Can Do With the PythonModule
11-3730 Plant Simulation Help
Unpublished work. © 2026 Siemens

To call Python functions in SimTalk with keyword arguments, use the method callKw:
print MyPythonModule.callKw("multiply", ["a", 3, "b", 4])
You have to pass keyword arguments using an array with pairs consisting of the keyword and the value.
The result looks like this when we run the Method.
With the Python code, which you type into the PythonModule, you can:
• Access the Model Frame from the Python Code
• Access Attributes and Methods from the Python Code
• Access DataTables from the Python Code
• Access Variables from the Python Code
• Call Global SimTalk Functions from the Python Code
• Execute External Python Code
You can also Display a PythonModule in the HtmlReport.
Note
Python code is not intended to replace SimTalk code. As SimTalk code also accesses compiled built-in
functions, it generally is executed quite a bit faster than Python code.
As many university graduates are exposed to Python, writing Python code during their studies, the
Python Module makes programming in Plant Simulation easier.
The presentation during the Plant Simulation User Meeting 2024 in München gives a good overview of
the Python Module. You can view and download it here Demo .spp model to use Python code in Plant
Simulation 2404 (siemens.com)
